# ---- audit tail: reading what the watch recorded (BH-444) ----
# bin/audit-tail.sh is the */5 operator line on every live box. It reads the
# records the installer above makes auditd write, collapses them into one burst
# per (pid, comm, class), writes every burst to logs/audit-tail.log and alerts
# through bin/lane-alert.sh. Reader and installer share AUDIT_ROOTS (above) and
# nothing else. Kill: AUDIT_TAIL=0, or the file flags/audit-off.
# Every value below is the code's REAL default; uncomment to override.
#AUDIT_TAIL=1
# Alerts per tick. The rest are logged and one extra line names how many were
# held back. The cap is spent CRITICAL FIRST, so an info flood cannot starve a
# page; the lane also never counts its own locks/, state/alerts/ or checkpoint.
#AUDIT_MAX_BURSTS_PER_TICK=5
# Events in one burst at or above this pages even when nothing about the paths
# is protected: one process deleting this many files under a root is a wipe.
#AUDIT_PAGE_MIN=25
# 1 = every burst pages, including a build releasing its own claim file. For an
# incident window, not for a normal week.
#AUDIT_PAGE_ALL=0

# Regenerate-rung opt-in for the blocked-resolver fix round (BH-968).
#BR_REGEN_RUNG=1
# How many LANDED regenerates one ticket may buy (BH-950.2/BH-970). The head8
# dedup in br_regen_offered is what bounds mc_rebase CALLS — one per distinct
# head — and this bounds the pushes. 0 is not a way to turn the rung off; use
# BR_REGEN_RUNG=0 for that.
#BR_MAX_REGEN=1

